// The `wac` command through three hosts, compared on stdout, stderr and exit code.
//
// **The first thing `issues/system/0230a` asks for, and the reason it asks for it first.** The
// operator ruled option 2 on 2026-08-21: the shared thing is the *wac program*, and Deno, Node and
// the native binary are hosts that hand it argv and capabilities. Under that ruling the two hosts
// cannot drift by construction — but "cannot drift" is a claim about a build, and this is the
// measurement. That issue's own words: *"Without it, 'shared semantics' is a claim, and the two
// loaders in 0229a are what a claim like that looks like after a few months."*
//
// `packages/wacc/example/wacc.wac` is the program. The native binary embeds it as a payload; the Deno
// and Node hosts get it through `packages/platform/build.ts`. All three parse the same argv in the same
// wac code, so what this file can still catch is the part *outside* the program: how a host reports an
// exit status, what it does with a stream, and whether a capability answers the same way.
//
// ## Except for `run`, where it is comparing two implementations rather than one
//
// **`run` is the first subcommand to live in the wac program rather than in a host**, moved there on
// 2026-08-22 under `issues/system/0230a` option 2 once `spawn` could start a module on every host
// (`issues/system/0144`). The native binary still answers `run` in Rust, *before* the payload sees it —
// so for those five rows the native column is `build_and_call` in `native/v8/src/main.rs` and the other
// two are `cmd == "run"` in `wacc.wac`. Two implementations of one command, held to the same output.
//
// That is the evidence step 4 of that issue asks for before either is deleted, and it is why the two
// were made to agree on their *usage* text as well: `wac run` with no entry says `run`'s own line on
// all three, and an unknown flag is named rather than becoming the entry, which took a change on each
// side. Four of the eleven rows exist only because the first attempt at each disagreed.
//
// **They still differ in one way no assertion here covers**: the Rust `run` instantiates the module in
// its own process, so the program writes to the real streams, while the wac `run` spawns it and relays.
// Same bytes on each stream and the same status; not the same thing to a program that wants a terminal.
//
// ## What is compared, and what is deliberately not
//
// Thirty-four invocations covering the six subcommands the program has plus `wac <module.wasm>`, each
// side's stdout and stderr concatenated, and the exit code. Not compared: timing, and the module bytes
// `build` writes — those have their own tests (`bindgenwac_test.wac` holds the two *generators* to the
// same bytes, which is a different claim from two hosts running one generator). The three hosts'
// `build` *output* is compared at the end of the test — one entry, the same basename in three
// directories — which was "worth adding and not here yet" from 2026-08-22 until it was added.
//
// One case is deliberately absent. A module whose manifest section is *present and not JSON* is
// reported with the parser's own text — `serde`'s on the native side, `packages/json`'s on the other
// two — and no wording either could adopt would be the other's. Every other failure here says the same
// sentence on all three, including "cannot read", which took `faultWords` on one side and
// `message_of` on the other to stop being an errno.
//
// ## What the grant rows are for
//
// `reader.wasm` and `reader-read.wasm` are **one program built twice**, differing only in a flag at
// *its* build. A module run as `wac <module.wasm>` gets what its own manifest declares — which is not
// `spawn`'s rule, where the parent decides — so the wac implementation has to read the manifest rather
// than ask for everything and let the host narrow. Asking for everything is the version that passes
// every other assertion in this file: with `declaredGrants` returning all five bits, `reader.wasm`
// prints `read ok` under a JS-hosted command built with `--allow-read` and `read denied` on the native
// binary. That is a capability hole rather than a difference, and these two rows are what see it.
//
// ## What it costs, measured 2026-08-25
//
// **56s wall and 57.6s of CPU, warm**, taken from a suite run rather than by hand — up from 2–3s, for
// three hosts instead of two and thirty-four cases instead of six. Twenty-eight of them run
// something — fifteen `test`, eight `run` and five naming a module directly — where five `check` and
// `bindgen` rows stop at the diagnostics and one names a subcommand that does not exist. Six `build`
// invocations besides: three writing the module fixtures, and three more comparing what `build`
// writes. (15 + 8 + 5 + 5 + 1 = 34, and the parts are written out because the first attempt at this
// sentence summed to 33: a `[a-z]+\.wasm` count missed `reader-read.wasm`.)
//
// It was 39s at twenty-six cases on 2026-08-22, so the eight rows added since cost about 2.1s each,
// which is the figure to price the next one with. Nearly all of it is the three hosts each compiling
// the entry a row names. `test` is the dearest row per case: it compiles the file *and* instantiates
// it, where `check` stops at the diagnostics. The build is the only expensive part
// and is paid by `builtByDeno`, which keys on the closure of `wacc.wac`, `compiler/`, `build.ts` and
// `packages/platform/host/`; under it, `build.ts` keys its own work on the content of every file it
// reads, so a rebuild after an unrelated change is a copy and a runtime start. The first run after
// `wacc.wac` changes pays two real builds, about 40s each when this was written. No lane declaration at
// that cost — and the number is dated because `issues/system/0230c` is what happens to costs that are
// not.
//
// **The canary is a successful invocation**, and it is not decoration. Writing this by hand first, in
// a shell that does not word-split an unquoted variable, every case became `wac "check src/main.wac"`
// — one unknown command, six times — and all six "agreed" on exit 2 with identical output. A
// differential whose cases have collapsed into one reports perfect agreement. So the first row must be
// exit 0, and a run where every row is the same non-zero code is a broken harness rather than a green
// result.

import { T } from "../../../wactest/src/assert.wac";
import { Change, Cli, Core, Exec, FileResult, Stat } from "std/platform.wac";
import { builtByDeno, wacBinary } from "../../../wactest/src/built.wac";
import { itoa } from "../../../fmt/src/itoa.wac";
import { contains, joined } from "../../../wactest/src/strings.wac";

const string ENTRY = "packages/wacc/example/wacc.wac";

string scratch(Cli cli, string name) {
  string dir = cli.cwd().wait() + "/.cache/parity-" + name;
  cli.remove(dir, true).wait();
  cli.mkdir(dir + "/src", true).wait();
  // A second source directory for the fixtures a *named* row uses and the directory rows must not
  // discover. See the note beside the `--allow-env` row.
  cli.mkdir(dir + "/capsrc", true).wait();
  return dir;
}

/** One file, and the write is checked: a fixture that failed to appear is not a disagreement. */
bool put(T t, Cli cli, string path, string text) {
  Change c = cli.writeFile(path, text.toBytes()).wait();
  t.isTrue(c.ok(), "could not write " + path + " — " + c.message);
  return c.ok();
}

/**
 * `(12 ms)` becomes `(N ms)` — the one figure two hosts are allowed to disagree about.
 *
 * A *map* rather than a strip, because the failure this row exists to catch is a host printing no
 * timing at all, and deleting the figure from both sides would call that agreement.
 */
string steadied(string s) {
  string out = "";
  i32 i = 0;
  while (i < s.len()) {
    if (s.slice(i, i + 1) == "(") {
      i32 j = i + 1;
      while (j < s.len() && "0123456789".indexOf(s.slice(j, j + 1)) >= 0) { j = j + 1; }
      if (j > i + 1 && j + 4 <= s.len() && s.slice(j, j + 4) == " ms)") {
        out = out + "(N ms)";
        i = j + 4;
        continue;
      }
    }
    out = out + s.slice(i, i + 1);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return out;
}

/**
 * `""` when the two are byte-identical, or where and how they first differ.
 *
 * A description rather than a bool, because "the artefacts differ" is not something anyone can act on
 * and "byte 41 differs: 3 and 7" points at a section.
 */
string sameBytes(u8[] a, u8[] b) {
  if (a.len() != b.len()) {
    return "lengths differ: " + itoa(a.len()) + " and " + itoa(b.len());
  }
  for (i32 i = 0; i < a.len(); i++) {
    if (a[i] != b[i]) {
      return "byte " + itoa(i) + " of " + itoa(a.len()) + " differs: "
             + itoa(a[i]) + " and " + itoa(b[i]);
    }
  }
  return "";
}

/** What one host said. */
struct Said {
  i32 status;
  string out;
}

/**
 * Run `argv` through a host, from `dir`, and take everything it said.
 *
 * A shell line because the working directory has to be the project's — `Cli.execWith` passes argv and
 * an environment and not a cwd, which `issues/system/0231c` prices — and `exec` at the front so the
 * status is the command's own rather than a shell's copy of it.
 */
Said say(Cli cli, string dir, string command, string[] argv) {
  string line = "cd " + dir + " && exec " + command;
  for (i32 i = 0; i < argv.len(); i++) { line = line + " " + argv[i]; }
  Exec r = cli.exec("/bin/sh", string[]("-c", line), u8[0]()).wait();
  if (!r.ran()) { return Said(-1, "the host did not start: " + r.error); }
  return Said(r.status, string.fromBytes(r.stdout) + string.fromBytes(r.stderr));
}

/** The invocations, as argument vectors. */
string[][] cases() {
  return string[][](
    string[]("check", "src/main.wac"),        // the canary: this one must succeed
    string[]("check", "src/bad.wac"),         // a type error, with a span and a caret
    string[]("check", "src/missing.wac"),     // a file that is not there
    string[]("nonsense", "src/main.wac"),     // an unknown subcommand, so the usage line
    string[]("bindgen", "src/lib.wac"),       // TypeScript glue
    string[]("bindgen", "src/lib.wac", "--js"),
    // **`run`, which is the first subcommand to live in the wac program rather than in a host.**
    // `issues/system/0230a` option 2 moved it there once `spawn` could start a module everywhere
    // (`issues/system/0144`), and the native binary still answers it in Rust — so for `run` these
    // rows compare *two implementations of one command*, not one implementation through two hosts.
    // That is the evidence step 4 of that issue wants before either is deleted.
    string[]("run", "src/hello.wac"),         // it runs, and its output is the program's
    string[]("run", "src/seven.wac"),         // ...and the exit code is the program's, not 0 or 1
    string[]("run", "src/hello.wac", "--", "--allow-read"),   // past `--`, a grant is an argument
    string[]("run", "--nonsense", "src/hello.wac"),           // an unknown flag, named
    string[]("run"),                                          // no entry, so `run`'s own usage line
    // **`wac <module.wasm>` — "run that program", the most basic thing the command does**, and it
    // worked on the native binary alone until 2026-08-22: the other two printed the usage block at
    // somebody holding a built program. The grants are the interesting part. A module run this way
    // gets what **its own manifest** declares, which is not `spawn`'s rule — so the two rows below
    // are one program built twice, and the only difference between them is a flag at *its* build.
    string[]("hello.wasm"),                   // it runs, and its output is the program's
    string[]("reader.wasm"),                  // built without the grant: denied on every host
    string[]("reader-read.wasm"),             // built with it: allowed on every host
    string[]("nosuch.wasm"),                  // a `.wasm` that is not there — a file, not a command
    string[]("notamodule.wasm"),              // a file that is there and is not a program
    // **`test`, one file — `issues/system/0230a` step 5.** The module is loaded and its `test*`
    // exports called, which `Cli.load` made expressible (`issues/system/0240c`): before it a wac
    // program could start a *program* and not call a *function*, and a test is a function.
    //
    // **The directory case is the wac program's too**, since 2026-08-22: it walks for `*_test.wac` and
    // builds one aggregate module, as the native binary does, so there are rows below for a file and
    // for a directory. This said it was "still the native binary's alone" for two days after it
    // stopped being true, twenty lines above the rows that compare it.
    //
    // **`--coverage` has a row now, and the row is the point.** Its counters are reached through
    // `__cov_init`/`__cov_len`/`__cov_get`, which the instrumentation injects and no source names, so
    // no manifest listed them and `Cli.call` — which finds an export's signature in the manifest —
    // answered "no export named `__cov_len`" for a function the module plainly exports. The wac
    // program refused the flag rather than print the zeroes it would otherwise get, and this comment
    // said so. `issues/system/0243c` is closed: the compiler declares the three it injected, and the
    // three hosts agree on `6 of 279 points (2%)` and every line of the table beneath it.
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "src/one_test.wac"),     // 3 pass, 2 fail, and which
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--filter", "passes", "src/one_test.wac"),
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--filter", "zzz", "src/one_test.wac"),   // matches nothing
    string[]("test", "--nonsense", "src/one_test.wac"),       // an unknown flag, named
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "src/missing_test.wac"),
    // **The two grant rows.** The first is the canary for `issues/system/0242c`: a test that writes,
    // on a run that granted only read, has to be *denied* — and "denied" rather than "failed", because
    // `Change` reading the fault off its own refusal is half of what that issue fixed. The second is a
    // run that granted nothing at all, where the test is skipped and named and the status is 4.
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "src/writes_test.wac"),
    string[]("test", "src/reads_test.wac"),
    string[]("test", "--allow-env", "capsrc/env_test.wac"),
    // **`test` granting the network**, which no row did: `--allow-net` was in six `test` rows' worth
    // of company in the table and in none of them. A dial to a closed loopback port, asserted to come
    // back as a refusal rather than merely a failure — the category `issues/system/0255c` added.
    string[]("test", "--allow-net", "capsrc/net_test.wac"),
    // **A trap on the failing path, which is the only path that prints what the trap said.** The rows
    // above cover a trap that *passes* — `test_traps_on_purpose` — and a pass prints nothing, so what
    // `trap "…"` wrote had never been compared between hosts at all. It did not match: the native
    // reads the message out of the module's `$trap$message`, and `Cli.call` reported the engine's
    // `unreachable` instead.
    //
    // Its own file, and one trap in it, on purpose. `issues/lang/0254c` is that nothing clears the
    // global between calls, so a second trap in the same module would report the first one's sentence
    // on every host — agreement, on a value that is wrong. A differential can pin that as easily as
    // anything else, and pinning it is how it would survive being fixed.
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "src/breaks_test.wac"),
    // **`--verbose`, which was accepted and ignored.** `unknownTestFlag` let it through — so it was
    // spelled correctly and did nothing — while the native printed a line per passing test. A flag
    // that is refused is a bad answer and a flag that is silently dropped is a worse one.
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--verbose", "src/one_test.wac"),
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--coverage", "src/one_test.wac"),
    // **A socket failure, as a category rather than a sentence.** `Socket` carried no fault until
    // `issues/system/0238c` and the `FAULT_*` vocabulary had nothing for a network failure until
    // `issues/system/0255c`'s first half, so this printed nothing on any host — three hosts agreeing
    // on an empty string, which is what a differential cannot see by itself. The absolute half is
    // `packages/platform/test/wac/hostfaults_test.wac`, which asserts the category is `FAULT_REFUSED`
    // rather than comparing hosts. This row is the other half: that they still agree once it is not
    // empty, including the wording, which comes from `faultWords` and so is the program's rather than
    // any host's.
    string[]("run", "--allow-net", "src/dial.wac"),
    // **`run` granting, and `run` granting nothing.** Until these, no `run` row granted anything at
    // all — the one that mentions `--allow-read` passes it *past* `--`, as an argument. `run` is the
    // other command written twice, so how each implementation narrows a child's world is two
    // implementations' worth of question, and `issues/system/0242c` is what happens when nobody asks
    // it. One program, run both ways, reporting what it was allowed to do.
    string[]("run", "--allow-read", "--allow-write", "src/caps.wac"),
    string[]("run", "src/caps.wac"),
    // **A directory, which is a different command underneath.** One aggregate module for the whole
    // walk (`issues/system/0192`), a block per file, and a summary whose buckets are the interesting
    // part: `ok`, `not run (--ignore)`, `with failures`, `needing a grant`, `with nothing matching
    // --filter`. Five of those distinctions were wrong in my first attempt and one — telling a file
    // that wants a *flag* from one that wants a *host* — was wrong in the native command too.
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--allow-write", "src"),
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--ignore", "src/one_test.wac", "src"),
    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--filter", "zzz", "src"));
}

/**
 * The two hosts answer the same thing for the same command line.
 *
 * `@/` is in the fixture on purpose: it is what `issues/system/0229a` was about, and the resolution it
 * needs happens in the wac program rather than in either host — so a host that handed the program a
 * different view of the project would show up here rather than in a report from outside.
 */
export string test_the_three_hosts_answer_the_same_command_the_same_way(Core core, Cli cli) {
  T t = T.create();
  string dir = scratch(cli, "hosts");
  bool wrote = put(t, cli, dir + "/wac.json5", "{}\n")
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/lib.wac", "export i32 two() { return 2; }\n")
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/main.wac",
           "import { two } from \"@/src/lib.wac\";\nexport i32 main() { return two(); }\n")
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/bad.wac", "export i32 f() { return \"no\"; }\n")
    // For `run`: one that says something and succeeds, and one whose exit code is neither 0 nor the
    // 1 and 2 every failure here uses — so a forwarded status cannot be confused with a diagnosis.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/hello.wac",
           "import { Core } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n" +
           "export i32 main(Core core) { core.log(\"ran\"); return 0; }\n")
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/seven.wac", "export i32 main() { return 7; }\n")
    // For `wac <module.wasm>`: one that reads a file, so the grants its *own manifest* declares can be
    // seen from outside, and a file whose name ends in `.wasm` and whose contents do not.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/reader.wac",
           "import { Cli, Core, FAULT_NOT_GRANTED, FileResult } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n" +
           "export i32 main(Core core, Cli cli) {\n" +
           "  FileResult f = cli.readFile(\"src/lib.wac\").wait();\n" +
           "  core.log(f.ok ? \"read ok\" : (f.fault == FAULT_NOT_GRANTED() ? \"read denied\"\n" +
           "                                                                : \"read failed\"));\n" +
           "  return 0;\n}\n")
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/notamodule.wasm", "not a module\n")
    // **The first row here that touches the network.** Port 1 on loopback: nothing listens, and
    // connecting needs no privilege — only binding does — so every host gets a refusal rather than a
    // hang. It prints the *category*, which is the thing three hosts had no reason to agree on.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/dial.wac",
           "import { Cli, Core, Socket, faultWords } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n"
           + "export i32 main(Core core, Cli cli) {\n"
           + "  Socket s = cli.connect(\"127.0.0.1\", 1).wait();\n"
           + "  core.log(s.handle < 0 ? \"dial: \" + faultWords(s.fault) : \"dial: connected\");\n"
           + "  return 0;\n}\n")
    // For `test`: one of each outcome the runner has to tell apart — a pass, a failing report, a
    // `test_traps_*` that traps as its name says, and one that returns instead, which is a failure
    // whose whole point is that an empty report from it must not read as a pass.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/one_test.wac",
           "import { Cli, Core } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n" +
           "export string test_passes(Core core, Cli cli) { return \"\"; }\n" +
           "export string test_fails(Core core, Cli cli) { return \"1 of 1 assertions failed\"; }\n" +
           "export string test_traps_here(Core core, Cli cli) { return \"\"; }\n" +
           "export string test_traps_on_purpose(Core core, Cli cli) { trap; }\n"
           // **The camelCase spelling of the same rule.** `wactest/README.md` documents the prefix as
           // "`test_traps_` (or `testTraps`)" and three implementations take both; the wac runner took
           // only the first, so this export was a pass on the native binary and a failure on the other
           // two. Nothing in the repository was named this way, which is exactly why the arm could be
           // written wrong and stay wrong -- it is here so that it is driven.
           //
           // A bare trap, like its neighbour: two traps in one module and neither saying anything, so
           // `issues/lang/0254c` cannot make one of them report the other's sentence.
           + "export string testTrapsOnPurpose(Core core, Cli cli) { trap; }\n")
    // One trap, saying something, under a name that does *not* start `test_traps_` — so it is a
    // failure, and a failure is what prints the sentence. See the row that uses it.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/breaks_test.wac",
           "import { Cli, Core } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n" +
           "export string test_falls_over(Core core, Cli cli) { trap \"the reason it stopped\"; }\n")
    // **The capability canary.** A test that writes, run on a read-only grant. `issues/system/0242c`
    // is the day this passed on the JavaScript hosts and left `leaked.txt` on disk: a loaded module
    // was handed the *command's* world, and a `wac` command holds every grant because it has to
    // compile and write. The row that would notice is one where a test asks for more than the run did.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/writes_test.wac",
           "import { Change, Cli, Core, FAULT_NOT_GRANTED } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n" +
           "export string test_writes_a_file(Core core, Cli cli) {\n" +
           "  Change c = cli.writeFile(\"leaked.txt\", \"leaked\\n\".toBytes()).wait();\n" +
           "  if (c.ok()) { return \"\"; }\n" +
           "  return c.fault == FAULT_NOT_GRANTED() ? \"the write was denied\" : \"the write failed\";\n" +
           "}\n")
    // **The environment grant reaching a loaded module.** `--allow-env` was in the list `test` accepts
    // and in no row, which is the state `--verbose` was in — `tools/wac/testflagrows_test.wac` is the
    // check that now names such a flag. The question it asks is `issues/system/0242c`'s: a test runs
    // inside a module the command *loaded*, and the world it gets is the one that was asked for.
    //
    // **Its own directory, and that is not tidiness.** Put in `src/` it joins the three rows that walk
    // a directory, where the run grants read and write and *not* env — and there the hosts disagree:
    // the native answers the null this capability documents, and the JavaScript hosts **trap**.
    // `issues/system/0256c`. Moving this file back into `src/` is the regression test for that issue,
    // and is what turns those rows red today.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/capsrc/net_test.wac",
           "import { Cli, Core, FAULT_REFUSED, Socket } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n"
           + "export string test_dials_a_closed_port(Core core, Cli cli) {\n"
           + "  Socket s = cli.connect(\"127.0.0.1\", 1).wait();\n"
           + "  if (s.handle >= 0) { cli.closeSocket(s.handle);\n"
           + "    return \"something is listening on 127.0.0.1:1\"; }\n"
           + "  return s.fault == FAULT_REFUSED() ? \"\" : \"the dial did not report a refusal\";\n}\n")
    // What a program is allowed to do, reported rather than assumed — `probe.wac`'s shape, inline,
    // for the two `run` rows that differ only in what they grant.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/caps.wac",
           "import { Change, Cli, Core, FAULT_NOT_GRANTED, FileResult } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n"
           + "export i32 main(Core core, Cli cli) {\n"
           + "  FileResult f = cli.readFile(\"src/hello.wac\").wait();\n"
           + "  string read = f.ok ? \"ok\" : (f.fault == FAULT_NOT_GRANTED() ? \"denied\" : \"failed\");\n"
           + "  Change w = cli.writeFile(\"caps.txt\", \"x\\n\".toBytes()).wait();\n"
           + "  string write = w.ok() ? \"ok\" : (w.fault == FAULT_NOT_GRANTED() ? \"denied\" : \"failed\");\n"
           + "  core.log(\"read=\" + read + \" write=\" + write);\n"
           + "  return 0;\n}\n")
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/capsrc/env_test.wac",
           "import { Cli, Core } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n"
           + "export string test_reads_the_environment(Core core, Cli cli) {\n"
           + "  u8[]? v = cli.env(\"PATH\").wait();\n"
           + "  return v is null ? \"PATH is not readable\" : \"\";\n}\n")
    // ...and one that only reads, for the run that grants nothing: a test wanting a capability it was
    // not given is *skipped and named*, not failed, because "you forgot a flag" and "your test is
    // wrong" are different answers.
    && put(t, cli, dir + "/src/reads_test.wac",
           "import { Cli, Core, FAULT_NOT_GRANTED, FileResult } from \"std/platform.wac\";\n" +
           "export string test_reads_a_file(Core core, Cli cli) {\n" +
           "  FileResult f = cli.readFile(\"src/lib.wac\").wait();\n" +
           "  if (f.ok) { return \"\"; }\n" +
           "  return f.fault == FAULT_NOT_GRANTED() ? \"the read was denied\" : \"the read failed\";\n" +
           "}\n");
  if (!wrote) { cli.remove(dir, true).wait(); return t.report(); }

  string native = wacBinary(cli);
  t.isTrue(cli.stat(native).wait().isFile, "the native binary is built: " + native);
  string[] grants = string[]("--allow-read", "--allow-write", "--allow-run", "--allow-env",
                             "--allow-net");
  string deno = builtByDeno(cli, ENTRY, "wac-deno-cli", grants, "deno", false);
  // **Node is the third host and not an afterthought.** It is the same wac program through a
  // different runtime, and the only thing that differs underneath is how a worker is made — so if a
  // difference between hosts is ever going to appear anywhere, this is a column that would show it
  // at no extra risk of a false alarm. Its `run` goes through `host/childWasmNode.ts` where Deno's
  // goes through `host/childWasm.ts`, which is the one place the two are not literally one file.
  string node = builtByDeno(cli, ENTRY, "wac-node-cli", grants, "node", false);
  t.isTrue(cli.stat(deno).wait().isFile, "the Deno-hosted command was built: " + deno);
  t.isTrue(cli.stat(node).wait().isFile, "the Node-hosted command was built: " + node);
  if (!cli.stat(deno).wait().isFile || !cli.stat(node).wait().isFile
      || !cli.stat(native).wait().isFile) {
    cli.remove(dir, true).wait();
    return t.report();
  }

  // **Built once, by the native binary, and handed to all three.** The same bytes everywhere, so a
  // difference in what a host does with a module cannot be a difference in the module. Not
  // `builtProgram`, which keys a cache by name under `.cache/` — these belong to this fixture, and one
  // of them exists only to be denied.
  Said mk = say(cli, dir, native, string[]("build", "src/hello.wac", "-o", "hello"));
  Said mkR = say(cli, dir, native, string[]("build", "src/reader.wac", "-o", "reader"));
  Said mkG = say(cli, dir, native,
                 string[]("build", "--allow-read", "src/reader.wac", "-o", "reader-read"));
  t.eqI32(mk.status, 0, "hello.wasm built: " + mk.out);
  t.eqI32(mkR.status, 0, "reader.wasm built: " + mkR.out);
  t.eqI32(mkG.status, 0, "reader-read.wasm built: " + mkG.out);
  if (mk.status != 0 || mkR.status != 0 || mkG.status != 0) {
    cli.remove(dir, true).wait();
    return t.report();
  }

  string[][] table = cases();
  i32 agreed = 0;
  i32 zeroes = 0;
  i32 sevens = 0;
  // The two `caps` rows, counted by what they *say*. Three hosts agreeing proves they agree; it does
  // not prove a grant changed anything, and a `run` that ignored grants on every host would answer
  // `read=denied` twice and pass every assertion in the loop below. See where these are checked.
  i32 granted = 0;
  i32 refused = 0;
  for (i32 i = 0; i < table.len(); i++) {
    Said n = say(cli, dir, native, table[i]);
    Said d = say(cli, dir, deno, table[i]);
    // A built Node program is not executable by itself; `node` runs it, as `spawn_test.wac` does.
    Said j = say(cli, dir, "node " + node, table[i]);
    string what = joined(table[i], " ");
    // Timing is the one thing the header says is not compared, and `--verbose` prints some. Mapped to
    // `(N ms)` rather than deleted: a host that printed no timing at all would pass a comparison that
    // stripped the figure from both sides, and printing it is half of what that flag is.
    string nOut = steadied(n.out);
    string dOut = steadied(d.out);
    string jOut = steadied(j.out);
    t.eqI32(d.status, n.status, "exit codes differ, deno vs native, for `wac " + what + "`");
    t.eqStr(dOut, nOut, "output differs, deno vs native, for `wac " + what + "`");
    t.eqI32(j.status, n.status, "exit codes differ, node vs native, for `wac " + what + "`");
    t.eqStr(jOut, nOut, "output differs, node vs native, for `wac " + what + "`");
    if (d.status == n.status && dOut == nOut && j.status == n.status && jOut == nOut) {
      agreed = agreed + 1;
    }
    if (n.status == 0) { zeroes = zeroes + 1; }
    if (n.status == 7) { sevens = sevens + 1; }
    if (contains(n.out, "read=ok write=ok")) { granted = granted + 1; }
    if (contains(n.out, "read=denied write=denied")) { refused = refused + 1; }
  }

  // **The canary.** A clean check has to succeed, or the cases have collapsed into one — see the
  // header. Nine of these exit 0: two `bindgen`s, one `check`, the two `run`s of a program that
  // returns 0, and the three modules run directly.
  t.eqI32(zeroes, 14, "fourteen of these thirty-four succeed on the native host; a different count " +
                     "means the argument vectors did not arrive as vectors");
  // **And one exits 7**, which no failure path here produces: it is `src/seven.wac`'s own return
  // value carried back through a spawn and out of the process. A `run` that reported its own success
  // instead of the program's would read as 0 and every other assertion would still pass.
  t.eqI32(sevens, 1, "`run src/seven.wac` forwards the program's exit code, not its own");
  // **The grant rows are asserted to differ from each other**, which the loop above cannot see. It
  // compares hosts, so a `run` that ignored `--allow-read --allow-write` everywhere would answer
  // `read=denied write=denied` for both rows and agree perfectly. One of each is the claim that the
  // flags reached the program.
  t.eqI32(granted, 1, "`run --allow-read --allow-write src/caps.wac` gets both");
  t.eqI32(refused, 1, "and `run src/caps.wac` gets neither — the grants are what differ");
  t.eqI32(agreed, table.len(),
          "all three hosts agreed on " + itoa(agreed) + " of " + itoa(table.len()));

  // **What `build` writes, which no row above can see.** Every row compares what a host *said*, and
  // the artefact is what the command is for: three hosts running one generator could drift in the
  // bytes while agreeing on every word they print. This file's header has said "worth adding and not
  // here yet" about exactly this since 2026-08-22, with the measurement done by hand.
  //
  // **The same basename in three directories**, not three names in one. `wasmName` is
  // `baseName(stem) + ".wasm"` taken from the `-o`, so the name travels *inside* the module and a
  // comparison across two of them fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the pipeline —
  // which is the mistake `tools/seed.sh` records at the top of itself.
  cli.mkdir(dir + "/h1", true).wait();
  cli.mkdir(dir + "/h2", true).wait();
  cli.mkdir(dir + "/h3", true).wait();
  Said b1 = say(cli, dir, native, string[]("build", "src/hello.wac", "-o", "h1/same"));
  Said b2 = say(cli, dir, deno, string[]("build", "src/hello.wac", "-o", "h2/same"));
  Said b3 = say(cli, dir, "node " + node, string[]("build", "src/hello.wac", "-o", "h3/same"));
  t.eqI32(b1.status, 0, "the native host built it: " + b1.out);
  t.eqI32(b2.status, 0, "the Deno host built it: " + b2.out);
  t.eqI32(b3.status, 0, "the Node host built it: " + b3.out);
  FileResult a1 = cli.readFile(dir + "/h1/same.wasm").wait();
  FileResult a2 = cli.readFile(dir + "/h2/same.wasm").wait();
  FileResult a3 = cli.readFile(dir + "/h3/same.wasm").wait();
  t.isTrue(a1.ok && a2.ok && a3.ok,
           "three artefacts: " + a1.error + " " + a2.error + " " + a3.error);
  if (a1.ok && a2.ok && a3.ok) {
    // Not `a1.bytes.len() > 0` as well: a build that wrote nothing would fail the status assertions
    // above, and an empty file compared with an empty file is the shape this is here to refuse.
    t.isTrue(a1.bytes.len() > 0, "and the native one is not empty");
    t.eqStr(sameBytes(a1.bytes, a2.bytes), "", "Deno's `build` writes the native host's bytes");
    t.eqStr(sameBytes(a1.bytes, a3.bytes), "", "Node's `build` writes the native host's bytes");
    // **And the comparison can fail**, which three assertions that pass do not show. `reader.wasm` is
    // a different program built earlier in this test, so a `sameBytes` that answered `""` for
    // everything — a loop that never ran, a length check reading the wrong array — would satisfy the
    // two above and be caught here. The oracle has to be able to disagree.
    FileResult other = cli.readFile(dir + "/reader.wasm").wait();
    t.isTrue(other.ok, "the reader fixture is there to compare against — " + other.error);
    if (other.ok) {
      t.isFalse(sameBytes(a1.bytes, other.bytes) == "",
                "two different programs do not have the same bytes");
    }
  }
  // **A directory test in a project that has never built anything**, which is the one shape the rows
  // above cannot produce: they run from `dir`, and by the time a `test <directory>` row is reached a
  // `build` row has already created `dir/.cache` for its own artefact cache. That order dependency hid
  // two failures until an external project hit them on 2026-08-25 — the aggregate could not be written
  // because `.cache/` did not exist, and naming the directory by an absolute path produced an import
  // of `..//abs/path/x_test.wac`, a doubled slash and a climb out of the project.
  //
  // Its own directory, made after everything above has finished with `dir`, so "never built anything"
  // is true of it.
  string fresh = dir + "/fresh";
  bool ready = cli.mkdir(fresh + "/t", true).wait().ok()
    && put(t, cli, fresh + "/wac.json5", "{}\n")
    && put(t, cli, fresh + "/t/x_test.wac",
           "export string test_two_is_two() { return 2 == 2 ? \"\" : \"no\"; }\n");
  t.isTrue(ready, "the fresh project was written");
  if (ready) {
    // **Granted, because the command's own capabilities are not a host's to differ on.** A hosted
    // `wac` is a program built once with grants baked in; the native binary takes them per
    // invocation. So `wac test <dir>` with no grants is a fair question to neither — the native
    // cannot read the directory to discover it is one and compiles it as a file, and the hosted one
    // walks it. These pass what the directory rows above pass.
    string[] g = string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--allow-write", "t");
    Said rel1 = say(cli, fresh, native, g);
    Said rel2 = say(cli, fresh, deno, g);
    Said rel3 = say(cli, fresh, "node " + node, g);
    t.eqI32(rel1.status, 0, "native, a fresh project and a relative directory: " + rel1.out);
    t.eqStr(steadied(rel2.out), steadied(rel1.out), "deno agrees on a fresh project");
    t.eqStr(steadied(rel3.out), steadied(rel1.out), "node agrees on a fresh project");
    // **One file, so no `──` header and no blank line**, which is what the native prints and what the
    // hosted one printed a header for until this block asked.
    t.isFalse(contains(rel1.out, "── "), "one file in a directory gets no header: " + rel1.out);

    // The same walk with `--coverage`, which did nothing at all on the hosted command until this
    // block asked: the run happened and no table was printed. It also has to be the *same* table —
    // narrowed to the files walked, not the whole graph, or it reads `2%` for a suite that covers its
    // own files well.
    string[] c = string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--allow-write", "--coverage", "t");
    Said cov1 = say(cli, fresh, native, c);
    Said cov2 = say(cli, fresh, deno, c);
    t.isTrue(contains(cov1.out, "branch coverage:"), "native reports coverage: " + cov1.out);
    t.eqStr(steadied(cov2.out), steadied(cov1.out), "deno's coverage table is the native's");
    t.isFalse(contains(cov2.out, "std/platform.wac"),
              "and it is the walked files, not the whole graph: " + cov2.out);

    // Absolute, and compared with each host's own relative answer rather than across hosts: the
    // per-file header names the path the caller typed, so the two forms differ on purpose. What must
    // hold is that both work — an absolute directory produced `..//abs/path` and failed to compile.
    Said abs2 = say(cli, fresh, deno, string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--allow-write", fresh + "/t"));
    Said abs3 = say(cli, fresh, "node " + node,
                    string[]("test", "--allow-read", "--allow-write", fresh + "/t"));
    t.eqI32(abs2.status, 0, "deno, the same directory named absolutely: " + abs2.out);
    t.eqI32(abs3.status, 0, "node, the same directory named absolutely: " + abs3.out);
    t.isTrue(contains(abs2.out, "1 passed, 0 failed"), "and it ran the test: " + abs2.out);
  }

  cli.remove(dir, true).wait();
  return t.report();
}
